  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Since $\u03a6(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t).$ Line $\\text{L1}$ identifies ${x}_{2}(t)$ as a column of $\u03a6(t),$ so ${x}_{2}(t)$ must satisfy the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $\u03a6(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since $\u03a6(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)],$ the derivative of $\u03a6(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column:\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating each column}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out\u00a0}A\\text{\u00a0on the left}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
